# Exercise: Two-Way Communication
#
# Strands can both send and receive. The cleanest pattern uses
# *two* channels — one for requests, one for responses.
#
# Why two? With a single channel, main would queue its request
# and could then `chan.receive` its own value before the worker
# strand had a chance to run (the scheduler is cooperative —
# strands only get CPU when the current one yields, and most
# blocking ops yield only when the queue is empty). Two
# channels remove the ambiguity: main only ever reads from the
# response channel, the worker only ever reads from the request
# channel.
#
# Closure capture works the same way it did in exercise 01,
# just for two channels instead of one. With `( request
# response )` on the stack, `[ body ] strand.spawn` captures
# both for the worker and leaves both untouched on main's stack.
#
# Your task: write `spawn-doubler` so it creates the two
# channels, spawns a worker that reads requests, doubles, and
# writes responses, and returns the two channels for main to
# use. Then the test below sends 21 and expects 42.
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: spawn-doubler ( -- Channel Channel )
    chan.make            # ( request )
    chan.make            # ( request response )
    # Your code here — spawn a worker whose body is the doubler.
    # Worker stack is ( request response ); receive a value from
    # request, double it, send the doubled value on response.
    # Drop the strand-id from `strand.spawn` so the two channels
    # are what's left on the stack.
;

: test-communication ( -- )
    spawn-doubler                # ( request response )
    over 21 swap chan.send drop  # send 21 to request: ( request response )
    chan.receive drop            # receive doubled, drop flag: ( request 42 )
    nip                          # drop the leftover request channel: ( 42 )
    # Do not edit below this line
    42 test.assert-eq
;
